{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R66-pending-b — the capability-chain depth bound.",
    "",
    "THE SECOND OBLIGATION UNDER §9.1's LAST ROW. Arch fixed the rule: a bundled row keeps its id for",
    "the first obligation and the rest APPEND from ECP-R99 -- so this one's number is arch's to",
    "allocate when the §9 conversion lands, and inventing one here would put a name in their namespace",
    "that nothing in their document backs. Routed as a finding, not split here (CQ-2, confirmed)."
  ],
  "id_status": "pending-split",
  "under": "ECP-R66",
  "split_index": "b",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F3 — to entity-system-architecture, unrouted as of this writing",
  "title": "A presented capability chain deeper than the peer's configured maximum is refused with 400 chain_depth_exceeded",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §4.10(b)",
  "spec_also": [
    "§5.5 (chain verification, the cost this bounds)",
    "§3.3"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "reading": [
    "§4.10(b): \"the peer MUST reject a presented chain exceeding its configured maximum depth with",
    "`400 chain_depth_exceeded` rather than walking an attacker-controlled chain unboundedly.\"",
    "",
    "THE STATUS IS THE REQUIREMENT, NOT DECORATION, and §4.10(b) is unusually explicit about why: \"The",
    "status is 400, not 403 -- a too-deep chain is a client-correctable structural excess, not an",
    "authorization denial; using 403 would conflate 'too deep' with 'you lack the capability' and the",
    "caller could not distinguish them.\" So a peer answering `403 capability_denied` on an over-deep",
    "chain is NON-CONFORMANT even though it refused the request, and a check asserting only \"the request",
    "was denied\" scores that peer green. The discriminating assertion is the (status, code) PAIR.",
    "",
    "THE REASON STRING IS EXCLUSIVE. §4.10(b)'s 0.8.1 §4a Ruling 3 disambiguation: this",
    "`chain_depth_exceeded` (400) is the CAPABILITY-chain depth limit, distinct from the CONTINUATION",
    "causal-depth brake, which suspends with `bounds_exceeded` (429). \"Two mechanisms, two reason codes --",
    "they MUST NOT share a reason string.\" A peer emitting 429/`bounds_exceeded` here has answered the",
    "wrong mechanism's code.",
    "",
    "MEASURABILITY. Unlike (a), the input is constructible only by a caller that can MINT a chain of",
    "arbitrary depth -- which needs a delegation path and therefore a posture that grants one. That is a",
    "precondition, declared below, and a run that cannot mint to depth+1 reports SKIP with the reached",
    "depth named. It is NOT a pass: \"could not build the input\" and \"built it and the peer refused\" are",
    "different results and collapsing them is the defect this repo exists to stop."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "a grant permitting delegation, sufficient to mint a chain of max_chain_depth + 1"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller",
      "delegatee-chain"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "declared_limits": [
      "max_chain_depth"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "declared_limits"
    ],
    "notes": {
      "max_chain_depth": "the peer's CONFIGURED maximum. 64 is §4.10's non-normative recommendation and MUST NOT be assumed — against a peer configured at 8, a depth-65 probe still exceeds the bound and the check passes for the wrong reason; against one configured at 256 it does not exceed it at all and the check passes having measured nothing."
    }
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "refused",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "an authenticated EXECUTE presenting a valid capability chain of depth max_chain_depth + 1",
      "why": "§4.10(b)'s MUST. The chain is otherwise VALID — every signature verifies, every link attenuates correctly — so the only property under test is its depth.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "mint_chain",
          "capture": "chain",
          "depth": "max_chain_depth + 1",
          "note": "if the peer refuses the MINT rather than the presentation, that is a different (and possibly also conformant) behaviour — record it and SKIP rather than scoring this row."
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "presented",
          "capability": "$chain"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "presented",
          "equals": 400,
          "why": "400, NOT 403 — §4.10(b) rules on this explicitly and a 403 conflates structural excess with authorization denial."
        },
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "presented",
          "equals": "chain_depth_exceeded",
          "why": "the decoded `result.data.code` (GUIDE-CONFORMANCE §5.2b.1), and not `bounds_exceeded` — that is the continuation brake's code and the two MUST NOT share a reason string."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same request presenting a valid chain of depth max_chain_depth exactly",
      "why": "MANDATORY. It proves the refusal discriminates on DEPTH and not on the chain being malformed, over-attenuated, or unauthorized for the resource. Without it, a peer that rejects every delegated chain passes the refusal arm.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "mint_chain",
          "capture": "chain_at_limit",
          "depth": "max_chain_depth"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "at_limit",
          "capability": "$chain_at_limit"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "at_limit",
          "not_equals": "chain_depth_exceeded",
          "why": "at the limit is not OVER it. Asserting 200 would add a resource-authorization precondition this requirement does not need; asserting 'not this code' is the whole of what the control must show."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that walks the presented chain without a depth bound",
      "expect": "O(depth) signature verifications proceed — the request is answered on its merits after unbounded work, or the peer stalls",
      "why": "the shape §4.10(b) exists to prevent; it is a CPU-exhaustion surface, not a hygiene preference."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "resource_bounds/r2_chain_depth_over_limit",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only."
  }
}
